<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ryazan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pereyaslavl-Ryazansky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ryazan is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the Oka river, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Called Pereyaslavl-Ryazansky until 1778, it replaced Old Ryazan, destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1237, as the seat of the princes of Ryazan in the fourteenth century. The principality was annexed by Moscow in 1521, and the city keeps its kremlin with the Dormition Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ryazan.jpg" alt="Ryazan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ted.ns · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A4%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B7%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%85%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%B2_%D0%A0%D1%8F%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ryazan stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka River</a> in Central Russia, 196 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and is the administrative centre of Ryazan Oblast. An older city, now called Old Ryazan, lay about 50 km east of the modern one and was the capital of the Principality of Ryazan until the Mongol invasion of 1237. The modern city was known as Pereyaslavl-Ryazansky until 1778, when it was renamed Ryazan by order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention of the city, under the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a>, dates to 1095. It became part of the Principality of Ryazan, which had existed since 1129 and was centred on the old city. Lying between woodland and steppe, the principality suffered invasions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> in the south, and Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> had burnt its capital several times by the end of the twelfth century.</p><p>Ryazan was the first Russian city to face the Mongol army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>. On 21 December 1237, after a short siege, it was completely destroyed and never recovered, and the seat of the principality moved to Pereslavl-Ryazansky, which took its name. In 1380 Grand Prince Oleg of Ryazan and his men were caught between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s coalition and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>'s army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a>.</p><p>The principality was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> in 1521. Its last ruler, Ivan V of Ryazan, was suspected of seeking a treaty with the Crimean Khanate and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, where he died no later than 1534. As the southernmost border of the Rus' lands, Ryazan kept suffering raids by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>. A railway connected it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1864, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it grew rapidly into an industrial, scientific and military centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ryazan is the seat of the Diocese of Ryazan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasimov">Kasimov</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>, with the Assumption Cathedral in the Ryazan Kremlin, which is a historic museum. The Pozhalostin Museum, one of the oldest art museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the memorial museum-estate of the physiologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-pavlov">Ivan Pavlov</a> are also in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ryazan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ryazan</b> fell in December 1237, Vladimir in February 1238 and Kiev in December 1240.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Daniel enlarged his modest appanage by taking Kolomna from the Principality of <b>Ryazan</b> around 1301 and inheriting Pereslavl-Zalessky in 1302.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1237 Batu Khan's armies returned, destroying <b>Ryazan</b> and Vladimir in 1237–1238 and Chernigov and Pereyaslavl in 1239, and in December 1240 they stormed Kiev.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tsardom of Russia grew out of the Grand Duchy of Moscow, which under Ivan III (r. 1462–1505) and Vasily III (r. 1505–1533) had absorbed Novgorod, Tver, Pskov and <b>Ryazan</b>, thrown off the overlordship of the Great Horde and become the dominant power among the Russian principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vsevolod the Big Nest defeated Gleb of Ryazan on the Koloksha in 1177, led a large expedition against the Bulgars in 1183, and in 1207–1208 crushed the princes of <b>Ryazan</b> and burned their capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short siege, the Mongol army of Batu Khan destroyed <b>Ryazan</b>, the first city of Kievan Rus' to be besieged in the invasion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
